# Normalize to LF in the repo; editors/tools may use platform endings locally.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# ── Binary media ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `binary` only tells git not to diff or munge these. It does NOT stop them
# bloating history: git keeps every revision of a binary forever, so a 20 MB
# walkthrough re-recorded ten times is 200 MB in every clone and every CI job,
# permanently.
#
# This repo is kept separate from the application precisely so media does not
# weigh down the application's history — which only pays off if it does not
# weigh down THIS one either. Git LFS keeps pointers in git and bytes in LFS storage,
# so a clone fetches only the revisions it checks out.
#
# ⚠ NOT ENABLED YET — the filter lines below are commented out because turning
# LFS on after large files are already committed means REWRITING HISTORY
# (`git lfs migrate import`), which invalidates every existing clone and open
# PR. Do it BEFORE the first heavy commit:
#     git lfs install
#     git lfs migrate import --include="*.mp4,*.webm,*.mov,*.png,*.jpg,*.webp"
#   then uncomment these and drop the plain `binary` lines.
#
# *.mp4  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.webm filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.mov  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.jpg  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.webp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mov binary
*.pdf binary

# SVG is text — diffable, tiny, usually hand-edited. Keep it out of the binary set.
*.svg -text
